## Caveats / known risks

### Finding: This is still a bus skeleton, not a bus executor.

Affected surface: Future Capability Bus / Router / Service branches.

Why it matters: Passing this branch proves bus-local validation/result/event skeleton behavior only. It does not prove action execution, routing, backend selection, cancellation propagation, stream binding, policy enforcement, event sink behavior, or graph integration.

Smallest safe correction: Keep execution work in a separate reviewed bus/router/backend branch.

What still needs verification: Future execution branches must prove behavior with targeted tests and, when runtime execution surfaces are touched, real-runtime checks as applicable.

---

### Finding: Event helpers require explicit event identity/time.

Affected surface: Future event sink / trace event branches.

Why it matters: This branch deliberately avoids hidden `Date.now()` / ID generation behavior. A future event sink may want generated IDs/timestamps, but that should be introduced with explicit ownership and tests.

Smallest safe correction: Keep generated event identity/time out of this skeleton branch.

What still needs verification: Future event branches should define the event ID/timestamp owner and event ordering semantics.

---

### Finding: Deprecated capability definitions reject at bus intake.

Affected surface: Future registry/versioning/router selection.

Why it matters: This is the simplest v1 intake rule. If future compatibility work needs deprecated definitions for migration or fallback, that should be a separate policy/router decision.

Smallest safe correction: Keep deprecated rejection in the v1 bus intake skeleton.

What still needs verification: Future router branches should decide whether deprecated capability support is ever allowed and under what policy.

